MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 5376919784957670863}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: b5bd5e60e42c9624b876a36ed587e020, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  infoTodisplay: The domestic cat (Felis catus) is a common house pet. With over
    85 million of these cats nationwide, many are becoming a common feature not only
    of our backyards and city streets but also of our parks and other wild lands.
    As a result many cats left within the Everglades are having a negative effect
    on the ecosystem as an invasive species. Unlike wild predators that switch to
    other prey or locations when food becomes scarce, domestic cats that are fed
    can afford to continue to hunt and kill prey even when prey populations decline.
    They are very effective predators on rabbits, squirrels, mice, lizards, snakes
    and many species of wild birds. A single individual free-ranging cat may kill
    100 or more birds and mammals per year. And cats compete with native wildlife
    and can spread disease. Outdoor cats have been identified as the primary host
    in the transmission of toxoplasmosis to wildlife, a disease which has caused
    death in manatees and other mammals. It is important to take matters to solve
    these problems to ensure the Everglades remain a balanced Ecosystem.
  textBoxToReveal: {fileID: 8608165835621983374}
  canvasBase: {fileID: 1536994414372335822}
  interactAudio: {fileID: 2769912649548635329}
  canvasPopupHeight: 5
